  "client_view_text": " \n## Step 1: Understanding the Basics of a Write-Ahead Log\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a mechanism used by databases to ensure data consistency and durability in the event of a crash or failure. When a database transaction is initiated, the database first writes a record of the transaction to the WAL before actually applying the changes to the database. This record is essentially a log of all transactions that need to be applied to the database.\n\n## Step 2: How the WAL Ensures Data Consistency and Durability\nThe WAL ensures that before any transaction is committed, its modifications are recorded in the log. This means that if the system crashes or fails before the transaction can be committed, the database can use the WAL as a recovery mechanism. The database can scan the WAL to identify the last committed transaction and then apply all transactions up to that point to restore the database to a consistent state.\n\n## Step 3: The Recovery Process After a Crash\nIn the event of a crash, after the database system is restarted, it checks the WAL for any uncommitted transactions. The database then reapplies these transactions, thus ensuring that the database returns to a consistent state as it was before the crash. This process is known as recovery, and it is crucial for"
}